\title{Decidability in Elementary Number Theory}
\taxon{Type Theory}
\date{2026-07-18}
\import{macros}
\p{
    This note follows Chapter 8 of [Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory](hott-2022).
    It separates constructive truth from algorithmic decidability and records the information
    carried by each decision.
}
\subtree{
    \title{Decidable propositions and equality}
    \transclude{def-0058}
    \p{
        A type #{A} has decidable equality when
        #{\Pi_{x,y:A}\mathsf{isDecidable}(x=y)} is inhabited. This is data: applying the
        decision procedure to two values performs a finite computation and returns evidence.
    }
    \p{
        Decidability is closed under negation, products, coproducts, and implication when the
        inputs are decidable. Finite dependent products and sums preserve it as well. These closure
        rules turn small decision procedures into larger ones without invoking excluded middle.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{Natural numbers}
    \p{
        Observational equality from the universe chapter yields decidable equality on
        #{\mathbb{N}}. Recursive comparison similarly decides #{m<n} and #{m\leq n}.
        Case analysis on a decision allows a construction to branch while retaining a proof of the
        chosen branch.
    }
    \p{
        The well-ordering principle says that an inhabited decidable subtype of #{\mathbb{N}}
        has a least element. One searches upward from zero; the existence witness guarantees that
        the search cannot continue forever. This is the constructive engine behind least divisors
        and greatest common divisors.
    }
}
\subtree{
    \title{GCD, primes, and Boolean reflection}
    \p{
        Since divisibility of natural numbers is decidable, bounded search constructs
        #{\gcd(m,n)} together with proofs that it divides both inputs and that every common divisor
        divides it. Searching for the least divisor greater than one supplies prime factors.
    }
    \p{
        Euclid's argument can then be read computationally: from any finite list of primes, form
        one plus their product and extract a prime divisor; it cannot occur in the original list.
        Thus the type expressing that there are arbitrarily large primes is inhabited.
    }
    \p{
        A decidable proposition can be reflected into a Boolean #{b} with
        #{P\leftrightarrow(b=\mathsf{true})}. Booleans are convenient for computation, while the
        reflected equivalence reconnects the result to proof-relevant reasoning.
    }
}
